        'publish_at' => '2026-11-09 09:00',
        'answer'     => 'A used car loan finances a previously owned vehicle, typically at a higher interest rate than a new car loan — roughly 11% APR on average in early 2026 versus about 6.4% for new cars. The exact rate depends heavily on your credit tier: borrowers with superprime credit might see rates near 6-7%, while subprime borrowers can see 19% or more. Terms commonly run 36 to 72 months, though lenders increasingly offer longer terms that raise the total interest paid.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'Used car loan rates run meaningfully higher than new car rates at every credit tier — roughly 11% average APR versus about 6.4% for new cars in early 2026.',
            'Your credit tier is the single biggest factor: superprime borrowers may see rates near 6-7%, while subprime borrowers can face 19% or higher.',
            'Nearly one-third of all auto loans now run longer than 6 years, which lowers the monthly payment but raises total interest and the risk of negative equity.',
            'Shopping your rate with 2-3 lenders before visiting a dealer, and keeping the term as short as you can afford, are the two biggest levers you control.',
        ],
        'content'    => '<p class="lead">A <strong>used car loan</strong> works the same way as a new car loan — the lender fronts you the purchase price, you repay it with interest over a fixed term — but the rate you get and the total you pay can differ substantially. Used car loans carried an average APR of roughly 11.4% in early 2026, compared to about 6.4% for new cars, and the gap between credit tiers is even wider than the new-vs-used gap itself. This guide covers what rate you can realistically expect, why used cars cost more to finance, and how to avoid the longest, costliest terms. Run your own numbers in our <a href="{tool:auto-loan-calculator}">auto loan calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Why used car loans cost more than new car loans</h2><p>Three factors drive the gap. First, default risk: used car buyers, on average, carry somewhat weaker credit profiles than new car buyers, who tend to skew toward higher incomes and stronger credit. Second, collateral value: a used car\'s resale value is harder to predict precisely than a new car fresh off the lot with a known MSRP, so lenders price in extra uncertainty. Third, remaining vehicle life: a used car has already absorbed its steepest depreciation, but it also has fewer years of useful life left, which raises the lender\'s risk if the loan term is long relative to how much life the car has left.</p>'
            . '<h2>Typical used car loan rates by credit tier (2026)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Credit tier</th><th>Approximate score range</th><th>Typical used-car APR</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Superprime</td><td>781-850</td><td>~6-7%</td></tr><tr><td>Prime</td><td>661-780</td><td>~8-9%</td></tr><tr><td>Nonprime</td><td>601-660</td><td>~13-15%</td></tr><tr><td>Subprime</td><td>501-600</td><td>~18-20%</td></tr><tr><td>Deep subprime</td><td>300-500</td><td>~21%+</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are approximate industry figures compiled from Experian-sourced reporting and will vary by lender, loan term, down payment, and the specific vehicle. Always get a real quote rather than assuming you\'ll land exactly on these numbers — the gap between a nonprime and subprime quote from the same lender can be several percentage points depending on the rest of your application.</p>'
            . '<h2>How loan term changes your total cost</h2><p>A longer term lowers your monthly payment but raises your total interest paid, sometimes dramatically. Experian\'s 2026 reporting found that nearly one-third of all auto loans now stretch longer than 6 years (72 months) — a meaningful shift from loan terms a decade ago, which more commonly topped out around 60 months. A longer term on a used car carries an extra risk: the car itself may wear out, need major repairs, or lose most of its remaining value before the loan is even paid off, leaving you financing a car that\'s worth far less than what you still owe.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: how term length changes the numbers</h2><p>$22,000 used car loan at 11% APR:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Monthly payment</th><th>Total interest</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>36 months</td><td>~$720/mo</td><td>~$3,920</td></tr><tr><td>60 months</td><td>~$479/mo</td><td>~$6,740</td></tr><tr><td>72 months</td><td>~$415/mo</td><td>~$7,880</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Stretching the same loan from 36 to 72 months roughly doubles the monthly affordability but also roughly doubles the total interest paid — a trade-off that makes sense for some budgets and not others. Compare your own numbers in the <a href="{tool:auto-loan-calculator}">auto loan calculator</a>, which also shows a full term-comparison table.</p>'
            . '<h2>How to get the best rate on a used car loan</h2><ul><li><strong>Get pre-approved before you shop.</strong> A rate quote from your bank or credit union gives you a real number to compare against dealer financing, and it tells the dealer you already have a backup offer, which can improve their offer.</li><li><strong>Check your credit report first.</strong> Errors on your credit report can push you into a worse tier than you actually qualify for; dispute anything wrong before applying.</li><li><strong>Keep your rate-shopping window tight.</strong> Multiple auto loan inquiries within about a 14-30 day window are typically counted as a single inquiry by credit scoring models, so shop several lenders in a short burst rather than spreading applications out over months.</li><li><strong>Put down what you can.</strong> A larger down payment reduces the amount financed and can sometimes move you into a better rate tier with the same lender.</li><li><strong>Don\'t automatically take the longest term offered.</strong> A lower advertised monthly payment is often achieved simply by stretching the term, not by getting a genuinely better deal.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Used car loan vs. refinancing later</h2><p>If your credit improves after you take out a used car loan — for example, after a year of on-time payments — refinancing into a lower rate can meaningfully cut your remaining interest, especially if rates have also dropped generally. Compare your current loan against current market rates using our <a href="{tool:refinance-calculator}">refinance calculator</a> before assuming your original rate is locked in for good.</p>'
            . '<h2>Dealer financing vs. bank or credit union financing</h2><p>Dealers often mark up the interest rate they receive from a lender before passing it on to you, which is a legal and common practice but one that can cost you real money if you don\'t have a comparison point. A pre-approved rate from your own bank or credit union acts as a ceiling: if the dealer can\'t beat it, you already have a good deal lined up. Credit unions in particular often post among the lowest average rates of any lender type for financing a used vehicle, though membership eligibility varies by institution.</p>'
            . '<h2>New car vs. used car: is the lower price always the better deal?</h2><p>A used vehicle usually costs less upfront, but the gap in borrowing cost can quietly eat into those savings. Someone financing a $22,000 used car at 11% pays noticeably more in interest, relative to the amount borrowed, than someone financing a $30,000 new car at 6.4% — even though the new car costs more overall. Run both scenarios through a calculator before assuming "used is automatically cheaper" once financing is factored in, especially if your credit tier pushes your used-car rate toward the higher end.</p>'
            . '<h2>How certified pre-owned (CPO) programs affect financing</h2><p>Certified pre-owned vehicles — used cars that have passed a manufacturer inspection and carry an extended warranty — sometimes qualify for promotional financing rates close to new-car rates, since the manufacturer is effectively vouching for the vehicle\'s condition. If you\'re comparing a CPO vehicle against a plain used car from a private seller, ask specifically whether CPO financing promotions apply; the rate difference can be large enough to offset a higher sticker price.</p>'
            . '<h2>What a pre-purchase inspection can save you</h2><p>An independent mechanic\'s inspection, usually $100-$200, can reveal problems that would otherwise turn a seemingly good loan into a bad financial decision — financing a car that needs $3,000 of repairs within six months effectively raises your real cost of ownership well above the loan\'s stated APR. This step matters more for private-party purchases, where there is no dealer reconditioning standard, than for dealer or CPO purchases.</p>'
            . '<h2>Watch for negative equity on trade-ins</h2><p>If you\'re trading in a car you still owe money on, and the trade-in value is less than what you owe, that gap — called negative equity — typically gets rolled into your new loan. This means you start the new loan already behind, paying interest on debt from a car you no longer own. If you\'re in this position, our <a href="/negative-equity-car-loan-how-to-get-out/">guide to getting out of negative equity</a> walks through your options before you roll it into another loan.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2><ul><li><strong>Focusing only on the monthly payment.</strong> A low monthly payment achieved through a long term can cost thousands more in total interest — always check the total interest figure, not just the payment.</li><li><strong>Skipping pre-approval.</strong> Walking into a dealership with no comparison rate puts you at a real negotiating disadvantage.</li><li><strong>Not checking for prepayment penalties.</strong> Most auto loans don\'t have one, but a small number do — confirm before planning to pay extra or pay off early.</li><li><strong>Financing add-ons you didn\'t ask for.</strong> Extended warranties, gap insurance, and other add-ons are sometimes bundled into the loan amount by default — ask what\'s included before signing.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Should you buy or lease instead?</h2><p>A used vehicle loan isn\'t your only path to driving a car you don\'t own outright. Leasing a new vehicle sometimes offers a lower monthly payment than financing a comparable used one, though you build no equity and face mileage limits. For most buyers focused on long-term cost rather than short-term payment size, financing a used vehicle you plan to keep well past the loan\'s payoff date remains the cheaper path over a 7-10 year ownership horizon.</p>'
            . '<h2>How your down payment interacts with your rate</h2><p>A larger down payment does two things at once: it shrinks the amount you\'re financing, which directly cuts your total interest paid, and in some cases it can nudge you into a better advertised rate tier with lenders who set minimum down payment thresholds for their best offers. Even an extra $1,000-$2,000 down, if you can manage it, often pays for itself many times over across a 60-month loan.</p>'
            . '<h2>What to do if you\'re quoted a rate that seems too high</h2><p>If a dealer or lender quotes you a rate noticeably above the typical range for your credit tier, ask directly what factors pushed it there — a thin credit file, a very long requested term, or a vehicle that\'s older than the lender\'s cutoff can all raise the quote beyond what your score alone would suggest. Getting a second and third quote from different lender types (bank, credit union, online lender) within the same short shopping window is the simplest way to confirm whether a quote is actually competitive or simply high.</p>'
            . '<h2>A quick glossary</h2><ul><li><strong>APR</strong>. Annual percentage rate — the full yearly cost of the loan, including interest, expressed as a single rate.</li><li><strong>Credit tier</strong>. A lender\'s categorization of your creditworthiness (e.g., superprime, prime, subprime), which drives the rate you\'re offered.</li><li><strong>Negative equity</strong>. Owing more on a car loan than the car is currently worth.</li><li><strong>Rate shopping window</strong>. The short period (commonly 14-30 days) during which multiple auto loan credit inquiries count as one for scoring purposes.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>Experian: automotive finance market reporting on average new and used car APRs and loan term trends; Consumer Financial Protection Bureau guidance on <a href="https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/understanding-vehicle-financing" rel="noopener" target="_blank">vehicle financing</a> and credit-inquiry rate-shopping windows.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal financial advice. Your actual rate depends on your credit profile, the lender, the vehicle, and current market conditions.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'What is a good interest rate for a used car loan in 2026?', 'It depends heavily on your credit tier. Superprime borrowers might see rates around 6-7%, while the overall average sits closer to 11%. Subprime borrowers can see 18% or more. Compare your own quote against these tiers to gauge whether you\'re getting a competitive offer.' ],
            [ 'Why are used car loan rates higher than new car loan rates?', 'Lenders price in greater default risk, less predictable collateral value, and a shorter remaining useful life for used vehicles compared to new ones — all three factors raise the rate at every credit tier.' ],
            [ 'What loan term should I choose for a used car?', 'The shortest term you can comfortably afford. Longer terms (72+ months) lower the monthly payment but substantially raise total interest and increase the risk of owing more than the car is worth.' ],
            [ 'Does shopping multiple lenders hurt my credit score?', 'Not much, if done within a short window. Auto loan inquiries made within roughly 14-30 days of each other are typically treated as a single inquiry by credit scoring models, specifically to encourage rate shopping.' ],
            [ 'Is dealer financing or bank financing better for a used car?', 'Compare both. Dealers can mark up the rate they receive from a lender, so a pre-approved rate from your own bank or credit union gives you a real comparison point and negotiating leverage.' ],
